  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Kai Strnad reported (and others have reported similar problems on the mailing list):",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "> Symptoms",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> We've been experiencing occasional losses of connections to fedora",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> during mass ingests. Ingests of seemingly random digital objects would",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> fail with a FilterEnforceAuthn Servlet throwing a 401 despite providing",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> the right credentials. After some debugging it dawned on me that there",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> might be a problem with the authentication and caching mechanism used by",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> Fedora.",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": ">",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> Findings",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> Thanks to the extensive logging capabilities I was able to trace down",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> this problem to the following situation:",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> The CacheEntry (expires every 10 minutes) is very close to expiration",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> and expires somewhere between the call to CacheElement.authenticate()",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> and the call to CacheElement.getNamedValues(). The CacheElement will be",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> repopulated as intended, but without the password. Now there is a new",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> CacheElement with everything populated but the password. Subsequent",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> calls don't set the password either, so it won't get set for the rest of",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> the authentication process which doesn't matter at this point because",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> the user has already been authenticated.",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> Subsequent authentications however will fail because the next call to",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> CacheEntry.authenticate() will compare the given password with the",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> password of the cache which will effectively compare the password with",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> null, resulting in the termination of the filter chain and the 401.",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> The bug will only appear if the EnforceAuthnFilter is part of the filter",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> chain (eg axis calls).",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": ">",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": ">",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> Possible Solution",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> The most obvious solution would be to supply the given password to the",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> new CacheEntry the same way it is done in CacheEntry.authenticate().",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> However, I don't know if there are side effects and I'm not that",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> familiar with the code...",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
